CORE
main.cpp
--cpp17
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ no copies: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ each object destroyed once: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.3\] line \d+ WRONG \(must FAIL\): FAILURE$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
^CONVERSION ERROR$
--
N5008 [dcl.init.general]/16.6.1, [class.temporary]/2, [stmt.return]/2: in
C++17 a prvalue of the class type initializes the result object directly
(`D x = D(4)', `D y = make(5)', `A a{make(6)}', array elements), so the
copy constructor is not called and no temporary is destroyed.  The front
end materialized a temporary, copied and destroyed it (bin53).
cpp_constructor now constructs the object in place from a constructor-
built prvalue of the same cv-unqualified class, and lets a call returning
it by value initialize it directly; the elements of the local array are
destroyed with it (cpp98_array_of_class_destroyed).  g++ 13 and clang++
18 accept this program, and it fails only its WRONG assertion.
